---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { byDrawer, pageOf, referencesIn, stats } from '../lib/divisions.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const groups = byDrawer('core');

/** Split a scope note so referenced codes render as links to their row. */
function segments(scope: string) {
  const codes = referencesIn(scope);
  if (codes.length === 0) return [{ text: scope, code: null }];
  return scope
    .split(/\b([1-5]\d{3})\b/)
    .filter((s) => s !== '')
    .map((s) => ({ text: s, code: /^[1-5]\d{3}$/.test(s) ? s : null }));
}

/** A cross-reference is local while it stays inside the tier this page publishes. */
function href(code: string) {
  const page = pageOf(code);
  return page === '/core' ? `#${code}` : `${base}${page}#${code}`;
}
---

<Base
  title="The core disciplines"
  description="The canonical four-digit index — five drawers and 46 disciplines, each with a scope note saying what files there and which discipline wins when two compete."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide cards · the canonical list</p>
    <h1>The core disciplines</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {stats.divisions} disciplines across {stats.drawers} drawers, every code ending in zero.
      This is the tier meant to be adopted whole: nothing in it depends on whose card box it
      is, and nothing in it is here because of one filer's interests. Cut a tab for each row
      and you have a working skeleton.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Read a code as <span class="ad">drawer</span> + <span class="ad">discipline</span> +
      <span class="ad">sub-discipline</span> — one digit, then two, then one. Two digits for the
      discipline leaves 99 slots per drawer, so a drawer you work in daily can keep sprouting
      for decades without a renumber.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Which is the whole reason this page can stay canonical.</strong> Every row here
      ends in zero, so every discipline carries nine unused units digits. Your own
      sub-disciplines go there — and a table you extend only in the fourth digit is a table
      whose codes never move.
      <a href={`${base}/extending`}>How to extend it →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The second column is the one that does the work. It answers <em
      >does this card go here?</em
    > rather than naming a field, and where two disciplines genuinely compete it says which one
    wins and sends you to the other.
    <a href={`${base}/find`}>Not sure which row? →</a>
  </p>

  {
    groups.map(({ drawer, rows }) => (
      <section class="drawer">
        <h2 id={drawer.code}>
          <span class="ad">{drawer.code}</span> {drawer.name}
        </h2>
        <p class="meta">{drawer.scope}</p>
        <ul class="divs">
          {rows.map((d) => (
            <li class={d.kind} id={d.code}>
              <span class="ad">{d.code}</span>
              <span class="t">
                <span class="name">{d.name}</span>
                <span class="scope">
                  {segments(d.scope).map((s) =>
                    s.code ? <a href={href(s.code)}>{s.text}</a> : s.text,
                  )}
                </span>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2>What is deliberately not here</h2>
  <p>
    No sub-disciplines. Not because they are unimportant — the opposite: they are the rows most
    worth having and the rows least possible to write for someone else. Nutrition under
    medicine, sales under business, note-taking under education are all defensible, and none of
    them is canon. They are anchors a particular person's cards asked for.
  </p>
  <p>
    So they are published separately, as one filer's worked example rather than as part of the
    list. Take this page as your skeleton, and let your own fourth digits arrive when cards ask
    for them.
    <a href={`${base}/extending`}>When a row is earned →</a>
    <a href={`${base}/extensions`}>See the author's {stats.personal} →</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    That the split is even possible is a finding, not a preference. Mapping the 2,576-entry
    academic outline onto this table reaches every one of the {stats.divisions} disciplines
    above — and misses two of the author's sub-disciplines entirely, because the academy has no
    entry for note-taking or for selling. The canonical tier is exactly the tier the academy
    can account for.
    <a href={`${base}/outline`}>The full mapping →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Why the numbers stop at four digits</h2>
  <p>
    A positional scheme — one digit per level, all the way down — would need about eleven
    digits, because one level of the source outline fans out to 130 siblings. A Dewey-style
    block allocation fares no better: nine slots per level, and it fails at the second level
    immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the digits stop at the discipline, and depth is carried by an unbounded alternating
    suffix instead. That is the trade the scheme makes, and everything else follows from it —
    including the fact that you will never renumber.
    <a href={`${base}/rules`}>How an address is computed →</a>
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .drawer h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .drawer h2 .ad {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .meta {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  .divs {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .divs li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    max-width: none;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .divs li:target {
    background: var(--tint);
  }
  .divs .ad {
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    flex: 0 0 3.2rem;
  }
  .divs .t {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .subdivision .t {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .subdivision .name::before {
    content: '└ ';
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .name {
    display: block;
  }
  .scope {
    display: block;
    font: 0.82rem/1.45 var(--serif);
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.1rem;
  }
  .scope a {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  @media print {
    .divs li {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
  }
</style>
